<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saddam Hussein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saddam Hussein was the Ba'athist ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, effective leader from the 1970s and president from 1979 to 2003. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> relied on the security services and repression, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anfal-campaign">Anfal campaign</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>. He led Iraq into war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> (1980–1988) and the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> in 1990. He was overthrown by the US-led invasion of 2003 and executed in 2006.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein.jpg" alt="Saddam Hussein" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iraqi News Agency · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saddam_Hussein_in_1998.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Saddam Hussein al-Majid was born on 28 April 1937 in the village of al-Awja, near Tikrit, to a peasant Sunni Arab family. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Arab Socialist Ba'ath Party</a> in 1957. In October 1959 he took part in the attempt to assassinate Prime Minister Abdul-Karim Qasim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>; he began shooting too early, which disorganised the ambush, and Qasim survived. In July 1968 he took part in the bloodless coup led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-hassan-al-bakr">Ahmed Hassan al-Bakr</a>, who became president with Saddam as his deputy. As vice president he nationalised the Iraq Petroleum Company and was involved in the Second Iraqi-Kurdish War of 1974 to 1975, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> were defeated and hundreds of thousands of Kurdish civilians were forcibly relocated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 16 July 1979 Saddam forced the ailing al-Bakr to resign and took the presidency, having long been the de facto leader. Days later he convened a Ba'ath assembly, claimed to have found a fifth column within the party and had 68 alleged conspirators named and arrested; 22 were sentenced to execution. In 1980 he ordered the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, fearing the spread of the Iranian revolution among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s Shia majority. The war ended after nearly eight years in a stalemate, with somewhere around a million lives lost.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> refused to waive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s war debt, Saddam accused it of slant-drilling Iraqi oil and occupied it, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-war">Gulf War</a> of 1990 to 1991. A coalition led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> defeated Iraq, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>. Uprisings of Shia Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> that followed were repressed, and the dead are estimated at 100,000 to 180,000, mostly civilians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and its allies invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> on 20 March 2003, accusing Saddam of developing weapons of mass destruction and of ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a>, claims that proved false. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> fell on 9 April, and he was captured on 13 December 2003. He was tried by the Iraqi High Tribunal for the killing of 148 people at Dujail in 1982, convicted on 5 November 2006 of crimes against humanity and sentenced to death by hanging. He was executed on 30 December 2006. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> praise him as a leader who stood up to Western intervention, while many Iraqis, especially Shias and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, remember a dictator responsible for repression.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saddam Hussein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saudi Arabia and Kuwait spent $25 billion backing <b>Saddam Hussein</b> in the Iran–Iraq War of 1980–1988, yet after Iraq's invasion of Kuwait in 1990 King Fahd invited American and coalition troops onto Saudi soil, and in 1991 Saudi forces flew bombing raids and joined the land invasion that…</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…finds personalist and military dictatorships more likely than democracies to start wars, since no voters or civilian elite can call their rulers to account, as <b>Saddam Hussein</b>’s invasions of Iran in 1980 and Kuwait in 1990 and the Argentine junta’s invasion of the Falkland Islands in 1982 illustrate.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Saddam Hussein</b> a smaller reproduction was built in Iraq as the entrance to a museum that has not been completed; it was completed in 1987, with the purpose of reconnecting to Iraq's history, and it has been damaged since the Iraq War.</blockquote><a href="/work/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>My Homeland, a poem by the Palestinian Ibrahim Tuqan of about 1934, served for much of the century as an unofficial anthem of the Palestinians, and in 2004 became the anthem of Iraq, after the anthem associated with <b>Saddam Hussein</b>'s government had been set aside.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ba'athist government was overthrown in 2003, that older piece was brought back for a short time in place of &quot;Arḍulfurātayni&quot;, the anthem in use since 1981 and associated with <b>Saddam Hussein</b>'s government, until Tuqan's song replaced it in 2004.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mawtini">My Homeland</a></li><li><blockquote>As US troops entered central Baghdad, a statue of <b>Saddam Hussein</b> in Firdos Square was toppled with the help of a Marine vehicle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tikrit, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in Camp Justice, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.6,43.68&amp;year=1981" aria-label="Tikrit on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5660 911 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saddam Hussein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6110 1181h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6110 1181h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.6,43.68&amp;year=1981">What was here in 1981? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
